WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:08.592
Look down a long hallway in an older game. Near your feet the floor tiles are crisp. Farther away, they melt into grey mush.

2
00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:14.830
Switch on the setting called anisotropic filtering, and the far floor snaps back into focus.

3
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:20.193
Why does a texture blur when you look along it, and what does that setting actually fix?

4
00:00:21.393 --> 00:00:24.551
Start with one pixel on the screen, a tiny square.

5
00:00:24.901 --> 00:00:34.991
Now follow it back onto the floor texture. On a floor seen at a low angle, that square lands as a long, thin shape. Long along your view, narrow across it.

6
00:00:35.341 --> 00:00:43.448
That shape is called the pixel's footprint. It is an ellipse, and its two radii are named a, the long one, and b, the short one.

7
00:00:43.798 --> 00:00:54.571
The math that turns the square into the ellipse is the Jacobian. In words, it records how far the texture moves when you step one pixel right, and one pixel down.

8
00:00:55.771 --> 00:01:02.513
Games blur distant textures with mipmaps, a stack of pre shrunk copies. Each level halves the size.

9
00:01:02.863 --> 00:01:10.521
The standard rule picks one blur level from the longest side of the footprint. In words, the level is the log base two of a.

10
00:01:10.871 --> 00:01:18.765
That blur is round. It blurs every direction by the long radius. Even across the floor, where the footprint was narrow.

11
00:01:19.965 --> 00:01:27.773
And here is the aha. The mush is not caused by distance. It is caused by using a circle where the footprint is an ellipse.

12
00:01:28.123 --> 00:01:34.480
The extra blur on the short side equals the log base two of a over b, counted in mip levels.

13
00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:42.873
For a footprint eight texels long and one texel wide, that is log two of eight, three whole levels of blur that nobody needed.

14
00:01:43.223 --> 00:01:52.545
Anisotropic filtering picks the level from the short side instead, and takes several samples along the long side. Sharp across, smooth along.

15
00:01:53.745 --> 00:02:00.892
On a real foreshortened floor, the ellipse changes from pixel to pixel. Here, colour shows how stretched it is.

16
00:02:01.242 --> 00:02:12.549
Near the camera it is almost a circle. Toward the horizon it stretches more and more. At this marked far pixel, it is about five point four five times longer than wide.

17
00:02:12.899 --> 00:02:19.064
Left, the round mip blur. Right, an ellipse aware filter. The check edges stay sharp on the right.

18
00:02:20.264 --> 00:02:25.427
To measure it, we shrink a test pattern of rings, with a footprint eight long and one wide.

19
00:02:25.777 --> 00:02:33.094
Without any blur, the fine rings fold into false stripes. That is aliasing, the crawling shimmer you see in games.

20
00:02:33.444 --> 00:02:39.183
The round mip removes the stripes, but also washes out the rings across the short side.

21
00:02:39.533 --> 00:02:52.951
The ellipse filter removes the stripes too, but keeps about twice as much detail across the short side. Eight point four seven eight, against four point two four four, in our minor axis energy measure.

22
00:02:54.151 --> 00:03:03.751
Two sanity checks. When the texture is magnified, both radii below one, the two filters must give the same picture. Measured difference, zero.

23
00:03:04.101 --> 00:03:13.787
And as the stretch grows from one to sixteen, the gap between round and elliptical filtering grows with it. At one, they are identical circles.

24
00:03:14.987 --> 00:03:23.221
What this does not prove. The ellipse filter here is a reference written for the lab, not the exact method inside graphics cards.

25
00:03:23.571 --> 00:03:32.531
This software renderer's own sixteen times setting barely changed the image, so we make no claims about real hardware quality or speed.

26
00:03:32.881 --> 00:03:42.460
And on the full hallway photo, the difference is subtle, an average of about one percent. The test pattern carries the proof. All forty one checks passed.

27
00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:48.887
The takeaway. A pixel's footprint on a slanted surface is an ellipse, not a circle.

28
00:03:49.237 --> 00:03:57.066
Round mip blur uses the long radius everywhere, and over blurs the short side by log two of a over b levels.

29
00:03:57.416 --> 00:04:04.029
Anisotropic filtering follows the ellipse. That is why turning it on rescues the far floor in games.

30
00:04:04.379 --> 00:04:14.150
For further reading, see Williams on mipmaps, Greene and Heckbert on elliptical weighted average filtering, and Heckbert on the fundamentals of texture mapping.
